  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1238 AM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nFriday night through Wednesday:\n\n500 mb cluster analysis continues to show an anomalous ridge \naxis developing over the northern Plains into south central \nCanada this weekend into early next week. This should keep the \narea within northwest flow at 500 mb during this period. This \nmay allow for periodic chances for showers and thunderstorms \nlater this weekend into early next week, with a few shortwave \ntroughs possibly shifting through. \n\nWarm air advection in the low levels with south to southwest \nwinds developing this weekend should bring warm and humid \nconditions back into the region. There are small chances (around\n20 percent or less) for a few showers or storms to develop \nSaturday night within the warm air advection regime, though it \nmay be too capped per forecast soundings for anything to \ndevelop. Kept forecast dry for now. \n\nA warm front may move through the region Sunday or Sunday \nnight, which may bring a more organized period of showers and \nstorms. This potential may linger into early next week, until \nthe low pressure system shifts southeast by Tuesday. These rain\nchances should be periodic and not continuous. Given the\ninstability and shear within northwest flow aloft, there may be\nsome strong to severe storm potential Sunday into Monday night.\nDetails will hopefully become more clear in the coming days.\nEnsembles continue to keep the warm and humid conditions going\ninto next week, with rather small temperature spreads each day.\n\nWood",
